steps.body:
Some calls can fail at run time. `Integer.parseInt("abc")` cannot make a number, so it *throws* a `NumberFormatException`. Uncaught, that stops the program. Wrap the risky code in `try` and handle the failure in `catch`:

```java
try {
    int n = Integer.parseInt(text);
    sum += n;               // skipped when parseInt throws
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
}
System.out.println("still running");
```

When a line inside `try` throws, Java jumps straight to the matching `catch`, so the rest of the `try` block is skipped. After the `catch` finishes, the program continues with the statement after it. `e.getMessage()` returns the text that describes the problem, here `For input string: "abc"`.

Gotchas: `parseInt` rejects decimals (`"4.5"`), empty text, spaces (strip first), and values beyond the `int` range. It accepts a leading `+` or `-`. The binary form throws the same way: `Integer.parseInt("102", 2)` fails because 2 is not a binary digit. A variable declared inside `try` is local to that block, so declare totals before it.

example.code:
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        int sum = 0;
        int count = 0;
        String line;
        while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
            if (line.isBlank()) {
                continue;
            }
            try {
                int value = Integer.parseInt(line.strip());
                sum += value;
                count++;
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Sum of " + count + " numbers: " + sum);
    }
}

