steps.body:
One `try` can have several `catch` blocks. Java checks them top to bottom and runs the first one whose type matches. Put the most specific type first: a `catch (Exception e)` above `catch (ArithmeticException e)` is a compile error, because the second could never run.

```java
try {
    int result = values[index] / divisor;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {            // "/ by zero"
    ...
} catch (NumberFormatException | ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    ...                                      // one handler, two types
} finally {
    System.out.println("always printed");
}
```

A multi-catch `catch (A | B e)` handles unrelated types the same way. The types cannot be subclasses of each other: `catch (NumberFormatException | IllegalArgumentException e)` does not compile. `finally` runs after the `try` or the `catch`, whether or not something was thrown, and even when no `catch` matches and the exception keeps going. That makes it the place for cleanup and bookkeeping.

The common runtime errors and their messages:

- `ArithmeticException`: integer `/` or `%` by zero, message `/ by zero`. Double division never throws, it gives `Infinity` or `NaN`.
- `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`: a bad index, message like `Index 9 out of bounds for length 4`. A line with one token makes `parts[1]` throw this too.

example.code:
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class Main {
    static final int[] VALUES = {40, 75, 90, 12};

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        int lineNo = 0;
        String line;
        while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
            if (line.isBlank()) {
                continue;
            }
            lineNo++;
            String[] parts = line.strip().split("\\s+");
            try {
                int index = Integer.parseInt(parts[0]);
                int divisor = Integer.parseInt(parts[1]);
                int result = VALUES[index] / divisor;
                System.out.println(VALUES[index] + " / " + divisor + " = " + result);
            } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
                System.out.println("Math error: " + e.getMessage());
            } catch (NumberFormatException | ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
                System.out.println("Bad line: " + e.getMessage());
            } finally {
                System.out.println("Done with line " + lineNo);
            }
        }
    }
}

