class BankAccount {
    private int balance;
    private boolean closed;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (closed) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Account is closed");
        }
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive, got " + amount);
        }
        if (amount > balance) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not enough money, balance is " + balance);
        }
        balance -= amount;
    }

    public void close() {
        closed = true;
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

steps.body:
Every exception is a class. `RuntimeException` and its subclasses (all the ones so far) are **unchecked**: the compiler lets you ignore them. Other subclasses of `Exception`, like `IOException`, are **checked**: any method that can throw one must catch it or declare it with `throws`, or the code does not compile (`unreported exception ... must be caught or declared to be thrown`). That is why `main` has `throws IOException` when it calls `readLine`.

Make your own checked exception by extending `Exception` and passing the message to `super`:

```java
class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {
    InsufficientFundsException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

static void transfer(...) throws InsufficientFundsException { ... }
```

Every caller of `transfer` must now catch it or declare it too. Extend `RuntimeException` instead when callers should not be forced.

Try-with-resources opens a resource in parentheses and closes it automatically when the block ends, even when something throws:

```java
try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in)) {
    ...
}   // sc.close() runs here
```

`sc` exists only inside the block. Any class that has a `close` method from the `AutoCloseable` interface works here, like `BufferedReader`. Closing a Scanner on `System.in` also closes `System.in`, so read all your input inside that one block.

example.code:
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> balances = new TreeMap<>();
        balances.put("ana", 100);
        balances.put("ben", 40);
        balances.put("cy", 0);

        try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in)) {
            while (sc.hasNext()) {
                String from = sc.next();
                String to = sc.next();
                int amount = sc.nextInt();
                try {
                    transfer(balances, from, to, amount);
                    System.out.println("Sent " + amount + " from " + from + " to " + to);
                } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
                    System.out.println("Failed: " + e.getMessage());
                } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
                    System.out.println("Invalid: " + e.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println(balances);
    }

    static void transfer(Map<String, Integer> balances, String from, String to, int amount)
            throws InsufficientFundsException {
        if (!balances.containsKey(from)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown account " + from);
        }
        if (!balances.containsKey(to)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown account " + to);
        }
        int available = balances.get(from);
        if (amount > available) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException(from + " has " + available + ", needs " + amount);
        }
        balances.put(from, available - amount);
        balances.put(to, balances.get(to) + amount);
    }
}

class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {
    InsufficientFundsException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

